{
 "id": "hey-ho-nobody-home",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Hey, Ho, Nobody Home (round)",
 "description": "The round as Thomas Ravenscroft printed it in 1609, for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings the same short tune, entering a bar apart (tenor and bass an octave lower), and the harmony happens by itself. The words are the 1609 ones, \"fill the pot, Eadie\", in today's spelling.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "round",
  "early music",
  "folk"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 96,
 "key": "Fm",
 "timeSignature": "4/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "anonymous (printed 1609)",
  "tune": "anonymous, in Thomas Ravenscroft's Pammelia (1609)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella (round setting)"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: printed 1609",
  "tune": "public domain: printed 1609",
  "arrangement": "original round setting. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): a round, every part singing the same tune a bar apart in its own octave, and the tune's own range, an octave from C to C (Ravenscroft's, a minor third higher): the soprano's low C on \"home\", a tone under the beginner soprano range (three times); the alto's five seconds at the top on its high C through \"fill the pot, Ea-die\" the third time; the bass, singing it an octave down, has its top C on \"fill\", \"the\", \"pot\", \"Ea-\" and \"die\" a tone over the beginner bass range (three times, with five seconds at the top the third), so its middle sits high; and the alto's and tenor's fall of a fifth from C on \"die\" to F on \"Hey\", going on down by step to E flat on \"ho\", is the round coming back to its start.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Thomas Ravenscroft, Pammelia. Musicks Miscellanie (William Barley, London, 1609), No. 85, \"Hey ho no body at home\", a canon at the unison for five voices, in the alto clef, written here in F minor (a minor third higher) for up to four voices entering a bar apart; read from the facsimile at pbm.com/~lindahl/ravenscroft/pammelia/pam_44.gif (also IMSLP, Pammelia)"
 },
